  "starterCode": "import torch\n\ndef cv_window_partition(x: list, window_size: int = 2) -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Window Partition and Reverse** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef cv_window_partition(x: list, window_size: int = 2) -> dict:\n    t = torch.tensor(x, dtype=torch.float32)\n    B, H, W, C = t.shape\n    M = window_size\n    w = t.view(B, H // M, M, W // M, M, C).permute(0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5).contiguous().view(-1, M, M, C)\n    rev = w.view(B, H // M, W // M, M, M, C).permute(0, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5).contiguous().view(B, H, W, C)\n    return {'num_windows': int(w.shape[0]), 'window_shape': list(w.shape), 'is_reconstructible': bool(torch.allclose(t, rev))}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
